@page
@model SimulationViewer.Pages.YDriveModel

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>SMS &mdash; Driving Y Chromosome</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/site.css" />
</head>
<body>
    <div class="nav-bar">
        <a href="/" class="btn btn-back">&larr; All models</a>
        <span class="nav-title">Driving Y chromosome (X-shredder sex distorter)</span>
    </div>

    <div class="config-panel">

        <div class="config-section">
            <h3>Population &amp; Release</h3>

            <div class="param-row">
                <label for="generations">Generations</label>
                <input type="range" id="generations" min="5" max="100" step="1" value="30" />
                <span class="param-value" id="generations-val">30</span>
            </div>

            <div class="param-row">
                <label for="releaseNumber">Release males</label>
                <input type="range" id="releaseNumber" min="1" max="250" step="1" value="100" />
                <span class="param-value" id="releaseNumber-val">100</span>
            </div>

            <div class="param-row">
                <label for="mortality">Mortality rate</label>
                <input type="range" id="mortality" min="0.1" max="0.9" step="0.01" value="0.10" />
                <span class="param-value" id="mortality-val">0.10</span>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="config-section">
            <h3>Reproduction &amp; Fitness</h3>

            <div class="param-row">
                <label for="eggsPerFemale">Eggs per female</label>
                <input type="range" id="eggsPerFemale" min="1" max="200" step="1" value="50" />
                <span class="param-value" id="eggsPerFemale-val">50</span>
            </div>

            <div class="param-row">
                <label for="fitnessCost">Transgene fitness cost</label>
                <input type="range" id="fitnessCost" min="0" max="0.5" step="0.01" value="0.05" />
                <span class="param-value" id="fitnessCost-val">0.05</span>
            </div>
            <div class="migration-detail" id="fitness-detail"></div>
        </div>

        <div class="config-section">
            <h3>Sex Distortion</h3>

            <div class="param-row">
                <label for="xShredRate">X-shredding efficiency</label>
                <input type="range" id="xShredRate" min="0" max="1" step="0.01" value="0.95" />
                <span class="param-value" id="xShredRate-val">0.95</span>
            </div>
            <div class="migration-detail" id="shred-detail"></div>

            <div class="param-row">
                <label for="yDriveFertility">Drive male fertility</label>
                <input type="range" id="yDriveFertility" min="0.1" max="1" step="0.01" value="0.90" />
                <span class="param-value" id="yDriveFertility-val">0.90</span>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="config-section">
            <h3>Migration</h3>

            <div class="param-row">
                <label for="migrationBaseRate">Base migration rate</label>
                <input type="range" id="migrationBaseRate" min="0" max="0.5" step="0.001" value="0.100" />
                <span class="param-value" id="migrationBaseRate-val">0.100</span>
            </div>
            <div class="migration-detail" id="migration-detail"></div>
        </div>

    </div>

    <div class="action-bar">
        <button id="btn-run" class="btn btn-run" onclick="runSimulation()">Run Simulation</button>
        <a id="btn-view" href="/Viewer?model=ydrive" class="btn btn-view disabled">View Results</a>
    </div>

    <div id="progress-container" class="progress-container" style="display:none">
        <div class="progress-bar">
            <div class="progress-fill" id="progress-fill"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="progress-text" id="progress-text"></div>
    </div>

    <script>
        var SIM_MODEL = 'ydrive';
        var SIM_PARAMS = [
            'generations', 'releaseNumber', 'mortality', 'eggsPerFemale', 'fitnessCost',
            'xShredRate', 'yDriveFertility', 'migrationBaseRate'
        ];
    </script>
    <script src="/js/configure.js?v=4"></script>

    <div class="info-panel">

        <h2>About Driving Y Chromosomes</h2>

        <p>
            A <strong>driving Y</strong>, or <strong>Y-linked X-shredder</strong>, is a
            sex-ratio distorter: a nuclease inserted into the Y chromosome that is
            expressed during male meiosis and cleaves the X chromosome. In
            <em>Anopheles</em> the natural target is the highly repetitive ribosomal DNA
            block on the X, present in hundreds of copies, which is cut so many times
            that the chromosome cannot be repaired. Sperm that inherited the X therefore
            fail, while sperm carrying the Y &mdash; and the shredder along with it &mdash;
            are unaffected. A carrier male consequently sires almost exclusively sons,
            and every one of those sons inherits the driving Y.
        </p>
        <p>
            This gives the construct two reinforcing advantages. It is
            <em>over-transmitted</em>, because it is present in nearly every functional
            gamete instead of half of them, so it spreads even without any homing
            machinery. And it is <em>suppressive</em>, because the population it spreads
            through becomes progressively more male until too few females remain to
            sustain it. The idea is old: W. D. Hamilton (1967, <em>Science</em>)
            recognised that a driving sex chromosome could crash a population, and it
            remains one of the most theoretically robust genetic control strategies.
        </p>
        <p>
            Windbichler et al. (2008, <em>Nucleic Acids Research</em>) first showed that
            the homing endonuclease I-PpoI could be directed at <em>Anopheles gambiae</em>
            X-linked rDNA. Galizi et al. (2014, <em>Nature Communications</em>) then built
            a working synthetic sex distorter, achieving progeny that were roughly
            95% male, and Galizi et al. (2016, <em>Scientific Reports</em>) moved the
            construct onto the Y chromosome to make it a true driving Y. Simoni et al.
            (2020, <em>Nature Biotechnology</em>) later combined shredding with homing in
            a single &ldquo;sex-distorter gene drive&rdquo;. On the theory side, Deredec
            et al. (2011, <em>PNAS</em>) and Beaghton et al. (2016, <em>Genetics</em>)
            worked out the conditions under which a driving Y eliminates a population
            rather than reaching a stable, heavily male-biased equilibrium.
        </p>
        <p>
            A key attraction of this design is what it does <em>not</em> do. Homing drives
            create resistance alleles at their own target site every time repair goes
            wrong, and those alleles can end the drive. An X-shredder never converts an
            allele; it simply destroys gametes. Resistance would have to arise as X-linked
            variation that escapes cutting &mdash; much harder when the target is a large
            repeat array. That route is not modelled here, so the drive in this simulation
            cannot become resistant.
        </p>

        <h2>Parameter Guide</h2>

        <h3>Generations</h3>
        <p>
            The number of discrete, non-overlapping generations to simulate (5&ndash;100).
            Sex distortion acts fast once the driving Y is common, but the initial phase
            is slow, so allow 30&ndash;60 generations to see the full trajectory from
            release through to collapse or equilibrium.
        </p>

        <h3>Release Males</h3>
        <p>
            The number of driving-Y males released into Population 1 at generation 3
            (range: 1&ndash;250). Each carries one shredder Y and one wild-type X.
            Because the construct is Y-linked it can only ever be transmitted by males,
            so a release of males is the only option &mdash; there is no such thing as a
            driving-Y female. Larger releases shorten the slow initial phase.
        </p>

        <h3>Mortality Rate</h3>
        <p>
            Per-generation background mortality (0.1&ndash;0.9). It controls how many
            mate-search attempts each female gets: (1 &minus; mortality) &times; carrying
            capacity. This parameter interacts unusually strongly with sex distortion.
            As the population becomes male-biased, finding a mate gets <em>easier</em>
            for the few remaining females, which partly buffers the population against
            collapse.
        </p>

        <h3>Eggs per Female</h3>
        <p>
            Base fecundity per successful mating (1&ndash;200). This is the single most
            important determinant of whether sex distortion actually collapses the
            population, and it is worth understanding why. The population only starts to
            shrink once the surviving females cannot fill the carrying capacity, which
            needs (number of females) &times; (eggs) &times; (male fertility) to fall
            below 500. At 50 eggs per female, ten fertile females are enough to refill a
            population of 500, so the sex ratio has to reach roughly 98% male before
            numbers move at all. Drop fecundity to 10 and the same population starts
            declining at around 90% male. Realistic reproductive surplus is exactly what
            makes suppression hard.
        </p>

        <h3>Transgene Fitness Cost</h3>
        <p>
            Extra mortality (0&ndash;0.5) suffered each generation by an adult carrying
            the construct, applied before it reproduces. The readout under the slider
            translates the value into mortality figures for heterozygotes and
            homozygotes; for this model only heterozygotes occur, since the distorter
            sits on the Y and no individual has two Y chromosomes.
        </p>
        <p>
            This is <em>not</em> the same knob as drive male fertility above, and the
            distinction matters. Fertility acts on the <strong>number of eggs</strong> a
            drive male's mate produces &mdash; he survives and mates, but sires a smaller
            brood, which is what gamete loss to shredding looks like. The fitness cost
            acts on <strong>whether he is alive to mate at all</strong>, which is what
            nuclease toxicity and insertional disruption look like. Both reduce a
            carrier's contribution, but only mortality removes the driving Y from the
            population before it can be transmitted, so it is much more effective at
            preventing the drive from establishing.
        </p>
        <p>
            Because the driving Y is transmitted to nearly every offspring of a surviving
            carrier, this drive is unusually tolerant of a fitness cost: a male who
            survives passes it on almost regardless. That makes it a good model for
            testing how much cost a strongly over-transmitted element can absorb.
        </p>

        <h3>X-Shredding Efficiency</h3>
        <p>
            The fraction (0&ndash;1) of X-bearing gametes destroyed by the nuclease during
            meiosis in a drive male. The surviving gamete pool contains
            (1 &minus; efficiency) X-bearing sperm for every one Y-bearing sperm, so the
            probability that a given sperm carries the driving Y is
            1 / (2 &minus; efficiency). At 0 the Y segregates normally and half the
            offspring are male; at 0.95 about 95% are male; at 1.0 every offspring is a
            son. The readout under the slider shows the resulting male fraction as you
            move it. Published constructs sit around 0.95, and the region between 0.9 and
            1.0 is where the outcome changes from a male-biased but persistent population
            to outright elimination.
        </p>

        <h3>Drive Male Fertility</h3>
        <p>
            Fertility of a driving-Y male relative to a wild-type male (0.1&ndash;1),
            applied as a multiplier on the number of eggs his mate produces. This folds
            together two real costs: half the sperm complement is destroyed by the
            shredder, which matters whenever sperm are limiting, and the construct itself
            imposes a load through nuclease expression and off-target cutting.
        </p>
        <p>
            This parameter sets up the central tension of the design. A high fertility
            cost slows the driving Y's spread, because carrier males compete poorly for
            paternity against wild-type males &mdash; but it also deepens suppression once
            the Y has spread, because by then every male in the population is a carrier.
            Very low values (below about 0.3) can prevent the drive from establishing at
            all, so that the release simply washes out.
        </p>

        <h3>Base Migration Rate</h3>
        <p>
            Per-generation probability (0&ndash;0.5) that an individual moves between
            adjacent populations, decaying tenfold at each step along the chain:
            Pop 1&harr;2 gets the base rate, Pop 2&harr;3 base/10, Pop 3&harr;4 base/100,
            Pop 4&harr;5 base/1000. Migration is what lets the driving Y escape the
            release site &mdash; but note it also brings wild-type males back in, which
            dilutes the distorter locally and can rescue a collapsing population.
        </p>

        <h2>What to Look For in the Results</h2>
        <p>
            The <strong>sex ratio</strong> panel is where this drive shows itself first:
            the female fraction falls away from 0.5 as soon as the driving Y starts
            spreading. The <strong>adults</strong> and <strong>eggs</strong> panels lag
            behind it, staying flat while the reproductive surplus absorbs the loss of
            females, then falling sharply once that buffer is exhausted. The
            <strong>allele frequency</strong> panel tracks the distorter itself; because
            it is Y-linked, its frequency is scored across all sex chromosomes and so
            tops out near 0.5 rather than 1.0, since every individual also carries at
            least one wild-type X.
        </p>
        <p>
            Two outcomes are worth trying to produce deliberately. With high shredding and
            low fecundity you should be able to drive Population 1 to extinction while the
            far end of the chain remains untouched. With a heavy fertility cost you should
            be able to make the release fail outright, with the driving Y peaking and then
            being lost.
        </p>

        <h2>Fixed Model Assumptions</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Population carrying capacity:</strong> 500 individuals per population. Egg production above this cap is culled by density-dependent regulation (random selection of survivors).</li>
            <li><strong>Number of populations:</strong> 5, arranged in a linear chain.</li>
            <li><strong>Migration topology:</strong> Linear chain only (1&harr;2&harr;3&harr;4&harr;5), decaying 10&times; per step.</li>
            <li><strong>Replicates:</strong> 3 independent iterations per simulation run, each starting from a fresh wild-type population.</li>
            <li><strong>Release timing:</strong> Driving-Y males are released once, at generation 3, into Population 1 only.</li>
            <li><strong>Sex determination:</strong> XY system. Females are XX; males are XY. The presence of a Y chromosome determines maleness, and the shredder does not interfere with that &mdash; it biases which sperm survive, not how sex is read.</li>
            <li><strong>No X-linked resistance:</strong> The X target site cannot mutate to escape cutting. In reality resistant X haplotypes are the main evolutionary threat to this design, but modelling them would require tracking the repeat array itself.</li>
            <li><strong>No homing:</strong> The construct destroys gametes rather than copying itself, so no drive-resistant target alleles (R1/R2) are ever created at the distorter locus.</li>
            <li><strong>Y-linkage is absolute:</strong> The sex chromosomes do not recombine, so the distorter can never move onto an X.</li>
            <li><strong>Non-overlapping generations:</strong> All adults die after reproducing.</li>
            <li><strong>Mating system:</strong> Random mating. Each female mates once per generation; males can mate with many females, so a shortage of males is never limiting.</li>
        </ul>

    </div>

</body>
</html>
